 # Density Control and Densification
 
-Also called: Adaptive Density Control, Gaussian Splitting and Pruning, Densification Strategy
 
 ## Overview
-Density Control and Densification is the mechanism in 3D Gaussian Splatting that dynamically adjusts the number, position, and scale of Gaussian primitives during optimization to improve reconstruction quality while maintaining a fixed budget [S2]. It addresses the limitation of standard gradient descent, which can only move, stretch, or fade existing Gaussians but cannot create new ones in empty regions or cleanly remove useless ones [S1, unofficial]. By inspecting view-space positional gradients at fixed intervals, it triggers cloning for under-reconstructed regions, splitting for over-reconstructed regions, and pruning for low-opacity primitives [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its siblings by relying on a simple threshold-based heuristic on accumulated positional gradients and spatial extent, whereas alternatives like [OTS+SAtn Densification](1488-ots-satn-densification.md) use attention mechanisms, and [Loss Accumulation Guided (LAG) Densification](3298-loss-accumulation-guided-lag-densification.md) uses loss accumulation [S3].
