 ### [Adaptive Densification](3034-adaptive-densification.md)
-Adaptive Densification is a mechanism that dynamically adjusts the number, position, and scale of Gaussian primitives during optimization to improve reconstruction quality while maintaining a fixed budget. It addresses the limitation of standard gradient descent, which can only move, stretch, or fade existing Gaussians but cannot create new ones in empty regions or cleanly remove useless ones. By inspecting view-space positional gradients at fixed intervals, it triggers cloning for under-reconstructed regions, splitting for over-reconstructed regions, and pruning for low-opacity primitives. It differs from its siblings by relying on a simple threshold-based heuristic on accumulated positional gradients and spatial extent, whereas alternatives like [OTS+SAtn Densification](1488-ots-satn-densification.md) use attention mechanisms, and [Loss Accumulation Guided (LAG) Densification](3298-loss-accumulation-guided-lag-densification.md) uses loss accumulation.
