 ### [Loss Accumulation Guided (LAG) Densification](3298-loss-accumulation-guided-lag-densification.md)
-Loss Accumulation Guided (LAG) Densification is a density control strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that uses accumulated reconstruction loss maps to identify under-reconstructed regions, preventing unnecessary Gaussian growth in well-reconstructed areas. Unlike standard 3DGS which relies solely on positional gradient magnitude to trigger splitting or cloning, LAG introduces a dual-constraint mechanism that inhibits Gaussian generation in regions where the photometric error is already low, addressing the issue where large gradients in high-frequency textures lead to redundant primitive generation. It builds on [Density Control and Densification](5337-density-control-and-densification.md) by adding a loss-based gating criterion to the standard gradient-based densification logic.
