 ### [Gaussian Splitting and Pruning](3304-gaussian-splitting-and-pruning.md)
-Gaussian Splitting and Pruning is a deterministic density control method for 3D Gaussian Splatting that dynamically adjusts the number of Gaussian primitives by splitting those with high reconstruction responsibility and removing those with minimal contribution. It differs from heuristic-based densification (e.g., [3D Gaussian Splatting Adaptive Density Control](3526-3d-gaussian-splatting-adaptive-density-control.md)) by relying on statistical criteria derived from per-ray loss and weights, allowing for iterative, detail-oriented refinement after initial convergence.
+Gaussian Splitting and Pruning is a deterministic density control method for 3D Gaussian Splatting that dynamically adjusts the number of Gaussian primitives by splitting those with high reconstruction responsibility and removing those with minimal contribution. It differs from heuristic-based densification (e.g., [3D Gaussian Splatting Adaptive Density Control](3526-3d-gaussian-splatting-adaptive-density-control.md)) by relying on statistical criteria derived from per-ray loss and weights, allowing for iterative, detail-oriented refinement after initial convergence. Choose it when deterministic, statistically-grounded refinement is preferred over gradient thresholds.
 
 ### [3D Gaussian Splatting Adaptive Density Control](3526-3d-gaussian-splatting-adaptive-density-control.md)
-This method refines the Gaussian primitive set by removing unstable or low-contribution primitives that remain after the densification phase described in [Density Control and Densification](5337-density-control-and-densification.md). It distinguishes itself from sibling methods by combining per-pixel opacity gradients with training loss to identify primitives that are sensitive to optimization (unstable) or contribute little to the final image (low-contribution), rather than relying solely on static opacity thresholds or gradient magnitudes.
